          "ruleId": "CWE-327",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWS/JWT algorithm confusion via an incomplete asymmetric-key blocklist in HMACKey.__init__. The attacker fully controls the JWS header 'alg' (jose/jws.py:254 `alg = header.get(\"alg\")`, derived from the token at jose/jws.py:74 `_load(token)`), and that value selects the key CLASS used for verification: jose/jws.py:212 `jwk.construct(key, alg)` -> jose/jwk.py:76 `get_key(algorithm)` -> jose/jwk.py:34 `if algorithm in ALGORITHMS.HMAC` returns HMACKey. The only defense is a literal blocklist checked at jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:565 `any(string_value in key for string_value in invalid_strings)`, where invalid_strings (line 558) is exactly [b'-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----', b'-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----', b'-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----', b'ssh-rsa']. That list omits OpenSSH-format non-RSA public keys -- b'ecdsa-sha2-nistp256/384/521', b'ssh-ed25519', b'ssh-dss' -- and omits b'-----BEGIN EC PUBLIC KEY-----'. So an application that calls jwt.decode(token, pubkey) with an OpenSSH-format ECDSA or Ed25519 public key as the verification key passes the blocklist, the public key bytes become the HMAC secret, and an attacker who knows that public key (it is public) forges an arbitrary token by setting alg=HS256 and HMAC-signing with it. The identical 4-entry blocklist also exists in the fallback backend at jose/backends/native.py:39; jose/backends/__init__.py:28 shows CryptographyHMACKey is the preferred import and cryptography==42.0.4 is a declared Dependency, so cryptography_backend.py:558 is the live code path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. (1) Blocklist verbatim: cryptography_backend.py:558-563 contains exactly 4 byte-strings [b\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\", b\"ssh-rsa\"], and reading CryptographyHMACKey.__init__ in full (542-571) shows the substring test at 565 is the ONLY key-type defense before `self.prepared_key = key` at 571. (2) The omission is proven, not assumed: a repo-wide grep for `ssh-ed25519|ecdsa-sha2|ssh-dss|BEGIN EC PUBLIC KEY` returns ZERO hits in the entire tree, so OpenSSH-format ECDSA/Ed25519/DSS public keys and PEM EC public keys all pass the filter and become the raw HMAC secret. (3) Attacker-controlled alg genuinely selects the key CLASS: jws.py:74 `_load(token)` -> jws.py:254 `alg = header.get(\"alg\")` -> jws.py:263 `_sig_matches_keys(keys, signing_input, signature, alg)` -> jws.py:212 `jwk.construct(key, alg)` -> jwk.py:76 `get_key(algorithm)` -> jwk.py:34-35 `elif algorithm in ALGORITHMS.HMAC: return HMACKey`, with ALGORITHMS.HMAC = {HS256,HS384,HS512} at constants.py:52. backends/__init__.py:28 imports CryptographyHMACKey as HMACKey, so cryptography_backend.py is the live path. (4) I found one guard the analyst omitted, and it does not rescue the default path: jws.py:258 `if algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms: raise JWSError` blocks the attack for callers that pin algorithms, but jwt.decode declares `algorithms=None` (jwt.py:59) and forwards it unchanged (jwt.py:145), so `jwt.decode(token, pubkey)` skips the gate entirely. Exploitability is therefore conditional on two realistic caller facts (algorithms not pinned, and the verification key supplied in OpenSSH/EC-PEM rather than the blocked SubjectPublicKeyInfo PEM) -- which is why I rate HIGH, not CRITICAL. (5) Reachability verified myself in the graph: lines 542, 558, 565, 566 are all reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. (6) Version pinned to python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1), inside the CVE-2024-33663 affected range (\"python-jose through 3.3.0\"; fixed in 3.4.0); exploit_search was unavailable (\"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so the version comparison rests on the manifest evidence rather than a Metasploit module. Net effect: an attacker who knows the (public) verification key forges any token by setting alg=HS256 and HMAC-signing with the public key bytes -> full signature-verification bypass."
          },
