          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The JWS algorithm allow-list is only enforced when the caller explicitly supplies one, so the attacker-controlled header 'alg' is otherwise unrestricted. At jose/jws.py:258 the guard is `algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms` -> raise JWSError('The specified alg value is not allowed'); when the caller passes algorithms=None the entire check is skipped and the attacker-supplied alg from jose/jws.py:254 flows unvalidated into jose/jws.py:263 `_sig_matches_keys(keys, signing_input, signature, alg)` and thence jose/jws.py:212 `jwk.construct(key, alg)`. jose/jwt.py:145 `jws.verify(token, key, algorithms, verify = verify_signature)` forwards the caller's algorithms value straight through with no defaulting or validation, so jwt.decode(token, key) with the algorithms argument omitted gives the attacker free choice of verification algorithm. This is the amplifier that makes the incomplete HMACKey blocklist (separate lead) exploitable: it is what lets the attacker pick alg=HS256 against a key the application intended for RS256/ES256.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: the JWS algorithm allow-list is fail-open. jose/jws.py:253 declares `_verify_signature(signing_input, header, signature, key=\"\", algorithms=None)`, and the sole guard at jws.py:258 is `if algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms`. Because of the `is not None` short-circuit, passing algorithms=None skips the allow-list entirely, so the attacker-controlled `alg` read at jws.py:254 from the unauthenticated header (decoded by _load at jws.py:176-206, before any signature check) flows unvalidated into jws.py:263 `_sig_matches_keys(..., alg)` and then jws.py:212 `jwk.construct(key, alg)` -> jose/jwk.py:76 `get_key(algorithm)` (jwk.py:31-52), where `alg` directly selects the key class (HMACKey/RSAKey/ECKey). The public API reaches this with no defaulting: jose/jwt.py:59 defines `decode(token, key, algorithms=None, ...)` and jwt.py:145 forwards it verbatim as `jws.verify(token, key, algorithms, verify=verify_signature)`; a grep over all of jose/ shows no other site that defaults or validates `algorithms`. This is not a dead branch -- the project's own tests assert the permissive path verifies successfully (tests/test_jwt.py:59, :111 both omit `algorithms`). Two corrections bounding the analyst's framing: (1) alg=\"none\" is NOT exploitable, since constants.py:70 excludes NONE from ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED, so get_key(\"none\") returns None -> JWKError -> JWSError; this is key/algorithm confusion, not a trivial total bypass. (2) The live exploit is choosing alg=HS256 so a key intended for RS256/ES256 is handed to HMACKey as an HMAC secret -- jose/backends/native.py:39-50 blocklists only PEM \"PUBLIC KEY\"/\"RSA PUBLIC KEY\"/\"CERTIFICATE\"/\"ssh-rsa\" and misses OpenSSH ECDSA (ecdsa-sha2-nistp256), ssh-ed25519 and ssh-dss, so an app passing an OpenSSH-format EC/Ed25519 public key gets full token forgery. Version pinned exactly at python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1) with no in-tree patch. Exploit-reference grounding was attempted but unavailable (exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so no module/CVE version comparison is claimed; this verdict rests only on source and graph evidence I gathered. Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL because this is library code: exploitation requires the consuming application to omit the `algorithms` argument AND hold a key in a format that slips the native.py blocklist."
          },
