        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-208",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWE AES-CBC-HMAC authentication tag is compared with a plain, non-constant-time `!=` operator instead of a constant-time comparison: jose/jwe.py:247 `if auth_tag != auth_tag_check: raise JWEError(\"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\")` inside _decrypt_and_auth. For enc values in ALGORITHMS.HMAC_AUTH_TAG ({A128CBC_HS256, A192CBC_HS384, A256CBC_HS512}, constants.py:67) this byte-string comparison IS the sole integrity check, since auth_tag_check is freshly computed by _auth_tag() at jwe.py:239. Python's bytes `!=` short-circuits on the first differing byte, leaking the length of the correct tag prefix via timing and enabling byte-at-a-time tag forgery against an attacker-queryable decryption oracle. The library demonstrably knows the correct control \u2014 jose/backends/native.py:69 uses `hmac.compare_digest(sig, self.sign(msg))` for the JWS path \u2014 but it is absent here. (For enc in ALGORITHMS.GCM this line is inert, as jwe.py:242 sets auth_tag_check = auth_tag.)\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: jose/jwe.py:247 compares the attacker-supplied JWE auth tag against the freshly computed HMAC (jwe.py:239) with a plain `bytes` `!=`, not hmac.compare_digest. I verified this is the SOLE integrity check for enc in ALGORITHMS.HMAC_AUTH_TAG (constants.py:67) by reading the backend: CryptographyAESKey.decrypt (cryptography_backend.py:493-518) accepts `tag=` but in the non-GCM branch (507-514) only runs the CBC decryptor + PKCS7 unpadder and never uses it, and backends/__init__.py:22-25 binds AESKey to CryptographyAESKey so there is no authenticating alternative. The lead's GCM caveat is also correct (jwe.py:242 sets auth_tag_check = auth_tag, making line 247 a tautology there). The correct control exists elsewhere in the same library (native.py:69 hmac.compare_digest; cryptography_backend.py:604 h.verify), and a repo-wide grep for compare_digest|constant_time|bytes_eq finds nothing in jose/jwe.py \u2014 so this is a genuine deviation, not a stylistic choice. Reachability confirmed in both graph and source: the notEquals node at jwe.py:247 has reachable_from_entry=true/hop_distance=1, and public jwe.decrypt() reaches _decrypt_and_auth at jwe.py:185 on fully attacker-controlled token bytes. I downgrade severity from the analyst's HIGH framing: the leak is one CPython memcmp over 16-32 bytes, swamped by interpreter and HMAC cost, so remote byte-at-a-time tag forgery is theoretical; notably the louder oracle on this path is non-timing, since plaintext is decrypted before the check (line 246) and jwe.py:188-189 surfaces PKCS7 \"Invalid padding\" distinctly from \"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\". Advisory exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no exploit/version grounding was available either way (version pinned at jose/__init__.py:1 = 3.3.0, tree is post-3.3.0 as it carries the CVE-2024-33664 fixes)."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
