        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-327",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Algorithm-confusion guard is incomplete in BOTH HMAC key constructors: the asymmetric-key deny-list `invalid_strings` only covers RSA and x509 formats \u2014 [b\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\", b\"ssh-rsa\"] \u2014 at jose/backends/native.py:39 (HMACKey.__init__) and the identical list at jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:558 (CryptographyHMACKey.__init__). It omits OpenSSH ECDSA public keys (b\"ecdsa-sha2-nistp256\"/\"-nistp384\"/\"-nistp521\"), b\"ssh-ed25519\", b\"ssh-dss\", and SEC1/EC PEM (b\"-----BEGIN EC PUBLIC KEY-----\"). Because jose/jws.py:212 builds the key with the TOKEN-SUPPLIED alg (`key = jwk.construct(key, alg)` inside _sig_matches_keys, where alg comes from `header.get(\"alg\")` at jws.py:254), an application that verifies with a public key in any of those un-denied formats can be forced down the HMAC path: the attacker sets alg=HS256 and signs the token with the (public, attacker-known) key material, forging a valid signature. The kty/alg consistency check that would catch this (cryptography_backend.py:574, ecdsa_backend.py:77) runs only in _process_jwk for dict/JWK keys, never for raw str/bytes/PEM keys, so the deny-list is the sole control on this path.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. The HMAC algorithm-confusion guard is an incomplete deny-list and is the SOLE control on the raw str/bytes key path. (1) Both HMAC key constructors contain the identical 4-element list covering only RSA/x509 formats (native.py:39-44, cryptography_backend.py:558-563) and omit OpenSSH ECDSA/Ed25519/DSS public-key prefixes (ecdsa-sha2-nistp256/384/521, ssh-ed25519, ssh-dss). (2) The alg that selects the key class is attacker-controlled: jws.py:254 reads alg = header.get(\"alg\") from the token header and passes it to _sig_matches_keys, which calls jwk.construct(key, alg) at jws.py:212; jwk.get_key returns HMACKey for any HMAC alg (jwk.py:34-35) and instantiates key_class(key_data, algorithm) at jwk.py:79. So alg=HS256 plus a raw public-key string yields HMACKey(pubkey_bytes, HS256), letting an attacker sign with the public (attacker-known) material and have hmac.compare_digest succeed (native.py:68-69). (3) The kty/alg consistency check that would otherwise catch this exists ONLY inside _process_jwk (cryptography_backend.py:99/275/574, ecdsa_backend.py:77, rsa_backend.py:176, native.py:55), which is reached only via the isinstance(key, dict) branch (native.py:29-31, cryptography_backend.py:548-550) and never for raw str/bytes/PEM keys. (4) My own additional evidence: jwt.decode defaults algorithms=None (jwt.py:59), so the allow-list check at jws.py:258 is inert unless the app explicitly pins algorithms; and tests/algorithms/test_HMAC.py:15-30 asserts rejection of exactly those 4 strings including \"ssh-rsa\", proving OpenSSH-format public keys are within the control's intended scope and the ECDSA/Ed25519/DSS omission is a true gap. Graph confirms the sinks are reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. CORRECTIONS to the analyst: the claimed \"-----BEGIN EC PUBLIC KEY-----\" (SEC1/EC PEM) omission is NOT valid, since EC public keys serialize as SPKI \"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\" which IS on the deny-list; the real gap is the OpenSSH formats only. Severity downgraded to MEDIUM (analyst implied HIGH) because exploitation requires an app that both passes a public key in a non-SPKI-PEM/OpenSSH format and omits algorithms pinning, and python-jose only ever calls load_pem_public_key (cryptography_backend.py:87, 265), never load_ssh_public_key, so legitimate ES256 verification with such a key would already fail. Version pinned to 3.3.0 at jose/__init__.py:1. NOTE: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I cite no exploit module and have no catalog evidence of either exploitability or patching; the verdict rests entirely on source and graph evidence gathered here."
          },
          "locations": [
