          "ruleId": "CWE-1395",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Declared dependency `ecdsa` carries the constraint `!= 0.15`, which excludes exactly one release and permits every other version of python-ecdsa. python-ecdsa is subject to the Minerva timing/side-channel attack on ECDSA signing (CVE-2024-23342 / GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp), which affects ALL released versions and for which upstream has published NO fixed version (the maintainers documented it as a won't-fix, since the pure-Python implementation cannot be made constant-time). The package is genuinely loaded at runtime, not test-only: `import ecdsa` at jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py:3 and `from ecdsa.ecdsa import int_to_string as _long_to_bytes` at jose/utils.py:13, and jose/backends/__init__.py:20 selects `ECDSAECKey as ECKey` as the EC backend. Any deployment that signs with ES256/ES384/ES512 via the ecdsa backend leaks private-key bits through signing-time variation. This is a components-with-known-vulnerabilities finding and does not depend on any request flow. VERIFIER: confirm the advisory and the absence of a fixed version against a live advisory DB; the graph only grounds the name/constraint and the runtime import.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently and the structural substance holds. (1) `ecdsa != 0.15` is declared as a MANDATORY install_requires at setup.cfg:36 (mirrored requirements.txt:3), and the graph :Dependency node agrees exactly \u2014 the constraint excludes only release 0.15 and permits every other version. (2) The vulnerable primitive is present and live: jose/backends/ecdsa_backend.py:98-101 calls ecdsa.SigningKey.sign(), the exact operation the Minerva nonce-timing attack targets, and the graph marks that call reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1 (as it does SigningKey.from_secret_exponent at line 87). (3) I re-traced the full reach in real source: jwt.py:56 -> jws.py:160-165 (key.sign(signing_input)) -> jwk.py:40-43 (returns ECKey for ALGORITHMS.EC) -> backends/__init__.py:20 -> ecdsa_backend.py:99. (4) The decisive fact the analyst MISSED is what actually makes this live: `cryptography` is NOT a required dependency, only an optional extra (setup.cfg:44-45) plus a dev pin (requirements-dev.txt:5), so a default `pip install python-jose` installs ecdsa WITHOUT cryptography and the except-ImportError fallback becomes the active EC backend; tox.ini's py3X-base envs show the project deliberately supports that cryptography-free configuration. CORRECTIONS: backends/__init__.py:20 does not unconditionally select ECDSAECKey \u2014 line 18 prefers CryptographyECKey and line 20 is only the ImportError fallback; jose/utils.py:13 is likewise inside an `except ImportError:` block (line 12), not an unconditional import, so the analyst's 'genuinely loaded at runtime' phrasing was overstated even though the conclusion survives. I also moved the anchor from the bare import line to the actual constraint declaration. CAVEAT bearing on confidence: mcp__orion__exploit_search returned 'exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed' and I had no live advisory DB in this session, so the lead's specific request to verify 'all versions affected / no upstream fix' went unmet; the exact installed version also cannot be pinned (`!= 0.15` is a range, no lockfile). CVE-2024-23342 / GHSA-wj6h-64fc-37mp being an all-versions won't-fix is consistent with my training knowledge and with python-ecdsa's own documented lack of side-channel resistance, but that part is recollection rather than evidence I gathered here. Severity MEDIUM rather than higher: exploitation needs many observed signing operations with low-noise timing measurement, and the exposure disappears entirely when the cryptography extra is installed."
