          "ruleId": "CWE-327",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The string literal \"RSA1_5\" is placed in the library's accepted-algorithm data set, enabling RSAES-PKCS1-v1_5 key unwrapping to be selected by an attacker-supplied JWE header. Chain of literals: `RSA1_5 = \"RSA1_5\"` (jose/constants.py:32) -> `RSA_KW = {RSA1_5, RSA_OAEP, RSA_OAEP_256}` (jose/constants.py:54) -> `SUPPORTED = HMAC.union(RSA_DS).union(EC_DS).union([DIR]).union(AES_JWE_ENC).union(RSA_KW).union(AES_KW)` (jose/constants.py:70). So RSA1_5 is in SUPPORTED, unlike NONE which is confined to ALL. At decrypt time the algorithm is read straight out of the attacker's token header: `alg = header[\"alg\"]` (jose/jwe.py:97, reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1), and the padding object is chosen by that attacker-controlled string via `{ALGORITHMS.RSA1_5: self.RSA1_5, ALGORITHMS.RSA_OAEP: ..., ALGORITHMS.RSA_OAEP_256: ...}.get(algorithm)` (jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:238, reachable=true), where `self.RSA1_5 = padding.PKCS1v15()` (jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:223). The selected padding is then used for the private-key operation `self.prepared_key.decrypt(wrapped_key, self.padding)` (jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py:403, reachable=true, hop_distance=1), reached from `key.unwrap_key(encrypted_key)` (jose/jwe.py:141, reachable=true). PKCS#1 v1.5 decryption is the classic Bleichenbacher / Marvin padding-oracle primitive: an attacker who can submit chosen JWE tokens with `alg=RSA1_5` and distinguish padding-error from MAC-error responses can recover the wrapped CEK and, with enough queries, mount a decryption oracle against the RSA private key. Note jose/backends/rsa_backend.py:129 (`algorithm in ALGORITHMS.RSA_KW and algorithm != ALGORITHMS.RSA1_5`) shows the pure-python backend treats RSA1_5 as the ONLY supported RSA key-wrap algorithm, so the weak option cannot be avoided there.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently and confirmed. (1) RSA1_5 is genuinely in the accepted-algorithm set: constants.py:32 `RSA1_5 = \"RSA1_5\"` -> :54 `RSA_KW = {RSA1_5, RSA_OAEP, RSA_OAEP_256}` -> :70 `SUPPORTED = HMAC.union(RSA_DS)...union(RSA_KW).union(AES_KW)`, contrasted with :72 where NONE is confined to ALL. (2) The algorithm is taken straight from the attacker's token: jwe.py:97 `alg = header[\"alg\"]`, gated only by `if alg not in ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED` (:99), so `alg=RSA1_5` is accepted. (3) That string selects PKCS#1 v1.5 padding (cryptography_backend.py:223 `RSA1_5 = padding.PKCS1v15()`; :238-242 `.get(algorithm)`), used for the raw private-key op at :403 `self.prepared_key.decrypt(wrapped_key, self.padding)`, reached via jwe.py:141 `key.unwrap_key(encrypted_key)`. rsa_backend.py:129 confirms the pure-python backend permits ONLY RSA1_5 among RSA_KW, so the weak option cannot be avoided there.\n\nIMPORTANT CORRECTION to the analyst's reasoning, which I had to re-derive: the library DOES implement the RFC 3218 mitigation at jwe.py:148-159 (any unwrap failure -> cek_valid=False + correct-length random CEK + continue to full decrypt/auth), which would defeat the oracle exactly as the analyst described it. I verified the mitigation is nonetheless INCOMPLETE, so a padding oracle still exists by a different path: it normalizes only the padding-FAILURE branch. When PKCS#1 padding is CONFORMING but the recovered CEK has the wrong length (the overwhelmingly common case while hunting for conforming ciphertexts), cek_valid=True and execution proceeds into _get_encryption_key_mac_key_and_key_length_from_cek (jwe.py:253-260) -> jwk.construct(encryption_key_bytes, \"A128CBC\") -> CryptographyAESKey.__init__ raises JWKError(\"Key must be 128 bit for alg A128CBC\") (cryptography_backend.py:453-454), which jwe.py:188-189 `except Exception as e: raise JWEError(e)` re-raises with the message intact. Padding-INVALID instead yields a 32-byte random CEK (jwe.py:557) that builds a valid AES key and fails only the tag check -> JWEError(\"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\") (jwe.py:248). Two deterministic, distinguishable error messages = a Bleichenbacher/Marvin decryption oracle on the wrapped CEK, violating RFC 7516 s11.5's uniform-error requirement.\n\nVersion pinned exactly at 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1). exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I obtained NO exploit-reference signal either way; I neither claim a weaponized module exists nor that this is patched. Verdict rests solely on source I read. Analyst's claimed location constants.py:70 is accurate as the enablement point."
